        "original_id": "653c12d9-9c01-57e1-b478-ce0c354babce",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Mikhail Glinka, Jean-Baptiste Lully, Alexey Verstovsky, Mikhail Zagoskin, Louis XIV\n- Works: Askold's Grave\n- Roles: composers, instrumentalist, dancer, librettist, musical bureaucrat\n- Concepts: Russian classical music\n\nFacts:\n- Jean-Baptiste Lully was born Giovanni Battista Lulli on 28 November 1632 and later became an Italian-born French composer, instrumentalist, and dancer.\n- Jean-Baptiste Lully spent most of his life working at the court of Louis XIV of France.\n- Alexey Verstovsky was born on 1 March 1799 and was a Russian composer and musical bureaucrat.\n- Mikhail Glinka was born on 1 June 1804 and became the first Russian composer to gain wide recognition within Russia.\n- Askold's Grave is a four-act opera by Alexey Verstovsky with a libretto by Mikhail Zagoskin.\n- Alexey Verstovsky was a rival of Mikhail Glinka.\n- Mikhail Glinka is often regarded as the fountainhead of Russian classical music.\n- Jean-Baptiste Lully died on 22 March 1687, Alexey Verstovsky died on 17 November 1862, and Mikhail Glinka died on 15 February 1857.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
